    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nPorsche Center Lock Recall Welcomes 548 Additional 911 and 718 Vehicles\n33 photos\nPhoto: Porsche\nPorsche is urging the owners of 548 vehicles to keep the cars parked until a dealer checks their wheels. Per the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, the center locks can fracture, allowing the wheels to detach while driving. Filed under campaign 26V682, this recall expands on 24V809 from October 8, 2024.\nThe 548 additional vehicles were identified when Porsche matched supplier data against production dates and vehicle build records, a traceability exercise that revealed gaps the first investigation somehow missed. Anyway, the affected population comprises 2024 models exclusively.\nSubject models range from the Carrera GTS to the GT3 RS and Cayman GT4 RS. The Carrera GTS leads with 168 potentially affected units in the US market, followed by the Turbo S at 94, the Cayman GT4 RS at 73, the Spyder RS at 70, the GT3 at 50, the GT3 RS at 41, the S/T at 39, and the Turbo at 13.\nBefore going any further, we should explain that center locks hold the wheels to the hub in lieu of the classic five-lug pattern. The centerlock system is a motorsport-inspired affair that saves weight and looks much cooler on performance-oriented vehicles. In motorsports, center locks speed up pit stops.\nRegarding the center locks of the affected population, the clamping force is concentrated in a single component. When said component fractures, the wheel separates from the hub, increasing the risk of a crash. According to Porsche, owners may feel perceptible vibration before the wheel detaches from the hub.\nPhoto: Porsche Two field incidents in May 2026 and the month of August 2026, both involving fractured center locks, pushed the Zuffenhausen-based manufacturer back to the supplier. Thankfully for everyone, no injuries were reported.\nThe follow-up investigation with Hirschvogel, specifically the supplier of the central locking wheel component, found that previously unidentified material movements within the manufacturing and logistics processes had escaped the traceability assessment.\nHirschvogel and Porsche determined that additional suspect center locks came from the same material batch as the first recall, with both parties identifying specific delivery notes tied to that batch. Subject center locks were used on 2024 models produced between June 7, 2024, and July 19, 2024.\nThe fix is the same as before, with dealers required to inspect the fastening system. If anything seems wrong, potentially defective parts will be replaced at no charge to affected customers. The replacement center locks are said to come from a properly specified batch. Porsche will be reaching owners by email, push notification, and phone in the weeks following the 26V682 filing.\nFormal customer letters should follow in the next couple of months. In any case, all customers will be notified by the end of December 2026. In the meantime, owners are urged not to drive their vehicles. The easiest way for customers to determine right now if their 911s and 718s need the remedy is to run the 17-character VIN on the automaker's website or the NHTSA's portal.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche Porsche 911 porsche 718 recall safety\nDownload: Porsche centerlock wheels recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Dealer Sells a 1975 Pontiac Trans Am That Checks Almost All the Right Boxes, Numbers Matching",
